# Greet CLI

## What this area does today

`greet.js` prints `Hello, <name>!` for the given name, `Hello, world!` with no arguments, and `greet 1.0.0` when the first argument is `--version`. Observed on main at commit 195d864, Node.js v25.8.1, on 2026-09-27.

## Components and paths

| Component | Role | Location or reference | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| greet script | Reads the command-line arguments and prints the greeting or the version | `greet.js` | Code at commit 195d864 |
| Version constant | Holds the version string `1.0.0` | `VERSION` in `greet.js` | Code at commit 195d864 |
| Tests | Acceptance checks for the greeting and version output, using Node's built-in test runner | `tests/greet.test.js` | `node --test`: 4 of 4 pass on 2026-09-27 |

## Data and flow

The script reads `process.argv` after the script path. If the first argument is `--version`, it prints the version. If there are no arguments, it prints `Hello, world!`. Otherwise it joins all arguments with single spaces and prints `Hello, <joined arguments>!`. It writes to standard output only and stores nothing.

## Operational behavior

Needs Node.js. No npm packages, no install step, no configuration.

## Known gaps and uncertainty

- An empty name (`node greet.js ""`) prints `Hello, !`.
- `--version` is recognized only as the first argument. `node greet.js Sam --version` prints `Hello, Sam --version!`.

WI-002 covers both.

## Last verified

- Date and environment: 2026-09-27, main at commit 195d864, Node.js v25.8.1.
- Sources: `greet.js`, `tests/greet.test.js`, WI-001.
- Maintainer: Mike.

-----
#!/usr/bin/env node
// greet: prints a greeting, or its version with --version.

const VERSION = '1.0.0';

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const name = args.join(' ');

if (args.includes('--version')) {
  console.log(`greet ${VERSION}`);
} else if (name.trim() === '') {
  // No name, an empty name, or a name made only of spaces.
  console.log('Hello, world!');
} else {
  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);
}

